# The invariants a compiler cannot see (#82).
#
# A rule set as data rather than as code. Each rule is a block of fields ending
# at a blank line, and .github/invariants/invariants.sh is what loads it. A block
# missing any field is refused, which is a condition of #82: a rule with no
# stated failure it prevents is a rule somebody added and nobody argued with.
#
# The fields:
#
#   id        what the rule is called, in the verdict and in the refusal.
#   pattern   an extended regular expression. A tracked line under `paths` that
#             matches it is refused.
#   paths     a prefix of a tracked path. The subject is git's set, so a file on
#             disk that nobody added is not judged and is not reported clean.
#   except    a prefix inside `paths` the rule does not reach. Optional, and the
#             one rule that carries it says in its own `prevents` why.
#   grounds   the landed record the rule comes from. A rule nobody can trace to a
#             decision is a preference.
#   prevents  the failure it prevents, in one line.
#   fixture   a line of source that violates the rule. Every run judges this text
#             against the WHOLE rule set and requires the verdict to be exactly
#             this rule, so a rule proves it bites and proves it bites alone.
#   clean     a line of source that nearly violates it and must not. The
#             one-character mistake somebody actually makes is worth more than a
#             violation that could not have passed.
#
# THE SEED LIST IN #82 IS NOT WHAT IS BELOW. Two of the four seeds do not survive
# translation into a pattern and the reason is written on #82 itself rather than
# here; a third does not either, because
# docs/decisions/0003-what-the-core-does-not-do.md says in so many words that the
# drawing boundary cannot be expressed as data. What replaces them are rules the
# landed records do support, and the `grounds` line on each block is where that
# is checkable.

# THE LAST THREE BLOCKS ARE #77's AND THEY ARE A FLOOR RATHER THAN THAT BOUNDARY.
# The sentence above still holds: 0003 says the forbidden side cannot be written
# down, its own reversal condition rests on that, and nothing here contradicts it.
# What #77 asks for is narrower than the boundary and is expressible: a check that
# fails on A name from the forbidden side, on a dependency whose purpose is
# drawing, and on a sentence written for a person. Each of the three below holds
# what has been named rather than what exists, so a crossing written in words
# nobody listed walks past all three. That is why they are three rules in a
# register and not a boundary in code: adding a name is editing this file.
#
# THEY LIVE HERE RATHER THAN IN A SECOND READER, which is what #77's own first
# comment asked about. Two of the three properties are described by more than one
# issue on this board, and a rule somebody adds later would otherwise go into
# whichever reader they met first. One register with one loader means #73 adds
# entries beside these rather than a second mechanism behind a second promise.

id: no-filesystem-access
pattern: (^|[^A-Za-z_])(std::fs|fs::(read|write|create|remove|copy|rename|File|OpenOptions)|File::(open|create)|OpenOptions::new)
paths: src/
grounds: docs/decisions/0068-the-data-locality-position.md
prevents: The core choosing where a byte is kept. Every byte goes through a store the client supplied, so a file opened here is a location the core chose on a platform it does not know.
fixture: let f = std::fs::File::open("cache.bin")?;
clean: /// The client supplies the store; nothing here opens a file.

id: no-network-outside-the-transport
pattern: (^|[^A-Za-z_])(std::net|TcpStream|TcpListener|UdpSocket|ToSocketAddrs)
paths: src/
except: src/server/
grounds: docs/decisions/0027-the-transports-timeouts-and-connections.md
prevents: A second way out to a server. Every request goes through one transport that bounds an attempt with timeouts and reuses connections, and a socket opened beside it has none of that. The exception is the transport itself, which is where the one socket is allowed to be.
fixture: let s = std::net::TcpStream::connect(address)?;
clean: /// The transport in src/server owns every request; nothing here connects.

id: no-text-output
pattern: (^|[^A-Za-z_!])(println!|eprintln!|print!|eprint!|dbg!)
paths: src/
grounds: docs/decisions/0003-what-the-core-does-not-do.md
prevents: A core that logs. Diagnostics leave as events with fields, through the client's sink, and a line written here has chosen a destination on a platform the core does not know and on a shared device a place it cannot promise is private.
fixture: println!("signed in as {user}");
clean: /// A diagnostic event carries fields, and the client decides where it goes.

id: no-platform-clock
pattern: (^|[^A-Za-z_])(SystemTime::now|Instant::now)
paths: src/
grounds: docs/decisions/0102-the-clocks-every-deadline-is-measured-against.md
prevents: A deadline measured against a clock no test can move. All three clocks reach the core through one injected source, which is what makes a timeout test take microseconds rather than seconds.
fixture: let started = std::time::Instant::now();
clean: /// The source supplies steady, elapsed and wall; nothing here reads one.

id: no-unstated-unit-where-a-position-is-meant
pattern: (^|[^A-Za-z_])((fn ([a-z_]*_)?(position|duration)\([^)]*\)[[:space:]]*->[[:space:]]*&?(i|u)(8|16|32|64|128|size))|(([a-z_]*_)?(position|duration):[[:space:]]*&?(i|u)(8|16|32|64|128|size)))
paths: src/
grounds: docs/decisions/0056-the-unit-a-position-is-expressed-in.md
prevents: A plain number of unstated unit where a position or a duration is meant, which is the one condition of #56 that is a property of the interface rather than of a test. The unit then becomes whatever the first caller assumed, and two positions that took different routes are compared as though they had been in the same one, which is what 0058's disagreement rule rests on. A name that states the unit is untouched, because something has to write the number on the wire, and that is the near miss beside this rule.
fixture: pub const fn position(self) -> i64 {
clean: pub const fn position_ticks(self) -> i64 {

id: no-user-interface-dependency
pattern: ^name = "(winit|glutin|softbuffer|sdl2|gtk|gtk4|gdk|gdk4|glib|egui|eframe|iced|slint|druid|dioxus|tauri|fltk|conrod_core|qmetaobject|cocoa|cacao|core-graphics|skia-safe|femtovg|raqote|tiny-skia|piet|wgpu|vello|glium|kiss3d|nannou)"
paths: Cargo.lock
grounds: docs/decisions/0003-what-the-core-does-not-do.md
prevents: A windowing, drawing or user-interface library arriving in the graph, which is the third of the four steps 0003 names as the way the boundary is crossed by accident. The subject is the committed lockfile rather than the manifest, so a package pulled in by something else is judged the same way one written by hand is.
fixture: name = "winit"
clean: name = "winnow"

id: no-view-vocabulary
pattern: (^|[^A-Za-z_])([Ss]croll|SCROLL|[Ff]ocus|FOCUS|[Ww]idget|WIDGET|[Vv]iewport|VIEWPORT|[Cc]anvas|CANVAS|[Rr]ender|RENDER|[Pp]aint|PAINT)
paths: src/
grounds: docs/decisions/0003-what-the-core-does-not-do.md
prevents: The core learning the words a view layer thinks in. 0003 places focus and a scroll offset outside by name and says a core that learns any of them has learned the shape of one client's screen. The vocabulary is what is refused rather than the boundary, so a crossing written in a word nobody listed is not caught here.
fixture: pub fn scroll_offset(&self) -> u32 {
clean: pub fn preview_bytes(&self) -> &[u8] {

id: no-sentence-for-a-person
pattern: ^[[:space:]]*impl[[:space:]<].*[^A-Za-z_]Display[[:space:]]+for[[:space:]]
paths: src/
grounds: docs/decisions/0003-what-the-core-does-not-do.md
prevents: A type of this core carrying its own wording. 0003 places the wording of anything a person reads outside, error text included, and this trait is the language's own name for a value written out for a person to read. The sibling trait for a programmer is untouched, which is the one-character difference the near miss beside this rule holds.
fixture: impl core::fmt::Display for SecretStoreUnavailable {
clean: impl core::fmt::Debug for Measurement<'_> {

id: no-reporting-dependency
pattern: ^name = "(sentry|opentelemetry|opentelemetry_sdk|tracing-opentelemetry|datadog|dogstatsd|statsd|cadence|prometheus|metrics-exporter|bugsnag|rollbar|honeycomb|libhoney|segment-analytics|amplitude|mixpanel|posthog|google-analytics|appcenter|crashlytics|breakpad|minidump|crash-handler|sadness-generator)([-_][a-z0-9_-]*)?"
paths: Cargo.lock
grounds: docs/decisions/0068-the-data-locality-position.md
prevents: A telemetry, analytics or crash-reporting package arriving in the graph, which 0068 rules out and which is normally added as a dependency rather than as a decision, so the decision never gets made. The subject is the committed lockfile, so a reporter pulled in by something else is judged the same way one written by hand is. It is a name list rather than a purpose test: a reporter published under a name nobody has written here is not refused.
fixture: name = "sentry"
clean: name = "metrics"

id: no-logger-installed
pattern: (^|[^A-Za-z_])(set_logger|set_boxed_logger|set_logger_racy|set_global_default|set_default_dispatch)
paths: src/
grounds: docs/decisions/0243-the-means-a-certificate-is-validated-with.md
prevents: A logger registered behind the logging facade 0243 admits. That record narrows 0103's fourth refused behaviour from linking a facade to writing to a log, and the whole narrowing rests on the facade's default sink writing nothing, which stops being true the moment something is registered behind it. The second exit for the values 0071 classifies field by field is then open with nothing having caught the change, and a record two levels away saying the case was considered. It is a name list rather than a purpose test: the five names are the registration calls of the facade this graph carries and of its neighbour, so a registration written through a name nobody listed walks past it, and a logger implementation arriving in the graph is 0103's question rather than this rule's.
fixture: log::set_boxed_logger(Box::new(EventSinkLogger))?;
clean: sink.event(&event); // the sink is the client's, and no logger stands behind it
